lmn-gpo (1.10.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * FIX: the RADIUS CA for the teacher Wi-Fi was accepted without validation. read_cert_der()
    returned the raw bytes of ANY file that lacked a BEGIN CERTIFICATE marker, so pointing
    wlan_enterprise_ca_cert at a private key, a CSR or a text file produced a SHA-1 thumbprint
    over garbage. Windows compares that thumbprint against the RADIUS server certificate and
    then refuses to connect WITHOUT ANY PROMPT, because the profile deliberately sets
    DisableUserPromptForServerValidation - so the failure was invisible on both ends and
    looked like a RADIUS problem. The file is now parsed with openssl and a bad one is a hard
    error naming the file, with the linuxmuster export command in the message.
  * apply now prints the certificate it pins ("RADIUS CA pinned: subject=... SHA1 ..."), and
    a missing CA file makes the teacher Wi-Fi pack skip with a warning and a non-zero exit
    instead of aborting the whole run mid-way.
  * A missing CA file no longer RETIRES the teacher Wi-Fi GPO. Since 1.5.0 an unmet
    precondition deletes the pack's GPO, which is right when the operator switched a feature
    off but wrong for a mistyped path - a typo would have destroyed a working GPO. Failures
    that come from a missing FILE are now tracked separately and the GPO is kept, reported
    and left for the next run, exactly like the wallpaper case.
  * setup: the wizard shows 'lmnradius ca export --out ...' as the way to obtain the CA and
    immediately echoes the subject and thumbprint of whatever path was entered.
  * README (both languages): document the full linuxmuster teacher-Wi-Fi path - export the
    EAP CA, three site.yaml keys, one apply - with a table mapping each manual click in the
    Windows profile dialog to the setting the pack writes. Also state plainly that the teacher
    Wi-Fi cannot be up BEFORE login: it authenticates the user, and preLogon means the 802.1X
    handshake runs during the Windows logon, not that the link already exists at the logon
    screen. Only machine authentication would change that.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Thu, 13 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.10.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * New pack 10b-veyon-bandwidth-schule: cut the network load of Veyon classroom monitoring.
    Active automatically wherever Veyon is (requires: veyon), so no extra switch.
    Sets Master\ComputerMonitoringUpdateInterval to 2000 ms (Veyon default 1000),
    Master\ComputerMonitoringImageQuality to 3 = Low (default 2 = Medium) and
    Master\RemoteAccessImageQuality to 2 = Medium (default 0, which is genuinely LOSSLESS
    and makes a single remote-view session the biggest stream in the room). Roughly 3-5x
    less traffic in a busy classroom. All three are tunable in site.yaml via
    veyon_monitoring_interval_ms / veyon_monitoring_quality / veyon_remote_quality, with the
    enum clamped to 0-4 and the interval floored at 100 ms, because Veyon silently ignores an
    out-of-range value and falls back to its own default -- which would look like the pack
    doing nothing.
    Documented prominently, because it is the natural expectation and it is wrong: Veyon has
    NO "lower the resolution" setting. The Master receives every student's FULL framebuffer
    and only scales it down locally afterwards, and the thumbnail size is a per-user JSON
    value rather than a registry one, so smaller tiles save nothing on the wire. Frame rate
    and compression are the only real levers.
    Both keys are read by the Veyon MASTER, i.e. the teacher's PC, which therefore has to sit
    in the school's OU=Devices -- true when teachers use the ordinary classroom computers.
    Needs Veyon >= 4.8 on both sides; against an older client the master silently falls back
    to lossless.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.9.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * FIX (1.9.0 regression, release-blocking): with exactly ONE Wi-Fi network no profile was
    ever installed. The generated script emitted `$PROFILES = @( @(name, xml) )`, and
    PowerShell's array subexpression unrolls a single inner array, so $PROFILES became a flat
    two-element string array and $PROFILES[0][0] yielded the first CHARACTER of the SSID.
    13-wlan-enterprise ALWAYS has exactly one profile, so it was broken every time, as was
    any school with a single PSK network. Reproduced in PowerShell during review. The list is
    now a typed System.Collections.Generic.List[object] built with .Add(), which has no
    count-dependent behaviour.
  * FIX: the SSID was interpolated verbatim into the generated .ps1, so an SSID with an
    umlaut reintroduced exactly the codepage parse failure fixed in 1.6.1. Both the profile
    name and the XML now travel base64-encoded and are decoded at runtime; the script stays
    pure ASCII whatever the SSID contains.
  * FIX: the installer no longer blocks the boot. It called the full reconcile inline, which
    waits up to 30x2 s for WlanSvc and again for an interface -- a minute of synchronous
    startup-script time on every desktop that has no wireless adapter at all. The inline pass
    now runs with no waiting; the scheduled task does the patient work.
  * FIX: the 15-minute reconciliation never actually started. The repetition hung off the
    BootTrigger, but the task is registered BY the startup script, i.e. after boot already
    happened, so it would not fire until the next restart -- where re-registration reset it
    again. There is now a separate repeating time trigger. The task is also built with
    New-ScheduledTaskTrigger/Register-ScheduledTask instead of hand-written XML, which
    removes the Task Scheduler schema's element-order pitfalls entirely.
  * apply: ntp_mode is validated. An unrecognised or empty value used to fall through to
    NT5DS -- the mode 1.8.0 declared broken and silent -- and the inline fallback still said
    "nt5ds" after the default changed. Anything that is not "ntp" or "nt5ds" now warns and
    uses "ntp".
  * lmn-gpo-check.ps1: the W32Time policy-vs-service Type comparison is reported, not
    asserted. The policy branch and the service's Parameters key are related but not the same
    setting, so comparing them produced a false failure on correctly configured clients.
  * README: the feature tables still advertised NT5DS as the time-sync default, contradicting
    the detailed section since 1.8.0.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.9.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Wi-Fi profiles are now delivered by a self-healing scheduled task instead of a one-shot
    boot import. Observed in the field: a LINBO notebook had the 13-wlan-psk GPO applied and
    a wireless interface present, yet no profile at all. Cause chain, documented by
    Microsoft: netsh wlan add profile wraps WlanSetProfile, which REQUIRES a wireless
    interface GUID; the profile store lives per interface inside WlanSvc; WlanEnumInterfaces
    only sees interfaces that are present AND enabled; and WlanSvc is trigger-started. A GPO
    startup script therefore routinely runs before the NIC has arrived, netsh without
    interface= adds the profile to "all wireless interfaces" (the empty set), and the old
    script hid it completely behind SilentlyContinue plus | Out-Null without ever checking
    $LASTEXITCODE.
    The startup script now installs LMN-GPO-WlanProfiles (boot + every 15 min + logon), the
    same two-stage pattern already used by the boot-order pack. The worker starts WlanSvc and
    sets it to Automatic, re-enables an administratively disabled adapter, waits for an
    interface, imports every profile per interface with interface= set, checks the exit code
    and logs failures to %SystemRoot%\Temp\lmn-gpo-wlan.log.
    Note the honest limit: profiles cannot exist while no adapter is present or enabled,
    because the store is per interface. The guarantee is "present within ~15 minutes of an
    adapter being available".
  * Wi-Fi: profiles are re-imported on every reconcile rather than skipped when present, so
    a rotated passphrase actually reaches provisioned clients. A per-user profile of the same
    name (which makes WlanSetProfile fail with ERROR_ALREADY_EXISTS even with overwrite) is
    now deleted and the import retried. Connection priority is set explicitly, because netsh
    inserts each new profile at the TOP and the import order otherwise comes out reversed.
    The local copy under ProgramData is ACLed to SYSTEM+Administrators by SID, not by the
    localized group name.
  * scripts_ext: delete .ps1 files a GPO no longer references. A renamed script used to
    linger in sysvol forever -- unreferenced but world-readable, which for the Wi-Fi pack
    meant an old passphrase stayed exposed. Found live: lmgpo-wlan-psk.ps1 from before the
    1.1.0 rename was still sitting next to the current script.
  * lmn-gpo-check.ps1: replace "No Wi-Fi service/adapter" with a real diagnosis covering the
    four distinct states (no WLAN feature, no adapter, adapter disabled, WlanSvc stopped),
    plus the state of the LMN-GPO-WlanProfiles task and the tail of its log.
  * The release workflow now also generates the DYNAMIC Wi-Fi scripts from lmn_gpo/wlan.py
    and parses them, so the PowerShell that only exists at apply time is syntax-checked too.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 13:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.8.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Time sync now defaults to plain NTP (ntp_mode: ntp) instead of NT5DS. Field-verified
    failure: with NT5DS a domain member REJECTS any reply that is not signed with its
    computer session key, a Samba DC can only sign via ntpd's ntpsigndsocket, and that chain
    is broken on a stock Ubuntu 24.04 DC. The client then silently keeps "Local CMOS Clock"
    and drifts past the 5-minute Kerberos limit while the GPO itself reports as applied.
    Seen on a production site: correct GPO, healthy secure channel, w32tm /stripchart against
    the DC returning valid offsets -- and the clock still 307 s off.
    Two independent server-side causes, both documented in docs/RESEARCH.md: ntpd applies
    only the MOST SPECIFIC matching restrict entry and REPLACES its flags, so a bare
    "restrict <school-subnet>" strips mssntp for exactly the client subnets even with
    "restrict -4 default ... mssntp" present; and Ubuntu 24.04 ships ntpsec 1.2.2, whose
    MS-SNTP handling is reported broken (fixed upstream in 1.2.3).
    ntp_mode: nt5ds remains available for sites with a working signing chain. Trade-off,
    stated in the README: with plain NTP the time is not authenticated.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 11:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.7.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * FIX: lmn-gpo-check.ps1 aborted with "The operator '<' is reserved for future use" at the
    per-user Wi-Fi profile hint. The line used \" to embed a quote, but \" is not an escape
    in PowerShell -- the backslash stays literal and the quote ENDS the string, so <SSID> was
    then parsed as code. The hint no longer embeds quotes at all.
  * The release workflow now parses every shipped .ps1 with PowerShell's own parser
    (System.Management.Automation.Language.Parser) and fails with file and line on any syntax
    error. The previous static checks could not catch this class of bug: the line had an even
    number of quotes and was pure ASCII, so both existing guards passed.

