lmn-gpo (1.6.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * FIX: the shipped PowerShell scripts did not parse on Windows PowerShell 5.1. Without a
    byte-order mark, powershell.exe reads a .ps1 in the system codepage, so a UTF-8 em-dash
    (E2 80 94) decoded to a curly closing quote under CP1252 and ENDED the enclosing string
    early -- producing a cascade of "missing )" / "string is missing the terminator" parse
    errors and aborting the script. lmn-gpo-check.ps1 was unusable, and
    bootorder-pxe-first.ps1 carried the same characters inside strings, so the boot-order
    startup script was affected on clients too.
    All scripts/*.ps1 (and the generator in lmn_gpo/wlan.py) are now pure ASCII, which makes
    them independent of the client's codepage entirely.
  * The release workflow now fails if any shipped .ps1 contains a non-ASCII character, so
    this cannot regress.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 08:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.6.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * New automatic prerequisite check. Applier.preflight() resolves every security-filter
    token (filter_deny, filter_deny_read, filter_apply) against the directory before
    anything is written, and reports each one that matches no group.
    - `lmn-gpo doctor` evaluates it against the real site.yaml, names every affected pack
      and school, and now exits 1 when an EXCLUSION resolves to nothing. That is the
      dangerous direction: the GPO then applies to exactly the devices or users it was
      meant to spare. A failed `filter_apply` is reported as a warning instead, because it
      fails closed (the pack is skipped).
    - `lmn-gpo apply` prints the same block BEFORE the first change, so a typo in
      teachernb or a school without a d_nopxe group surfaces up front rather than in the
      per-pack output after the fact.
    - `lmn-gpo env` flags any school that has no noPXE group at all.
    This replaces the manual "check lmn-gpo env before rolling out" step.

